When all is hushed that breathes below,
And all is still above;
 When night has closed her curtains gray,
 The stars are out, but they can't see us now;
 And only we two watch together,
 Through the soft darkness slowly flowing

Here comes the lord, from his palace to court;
In robes imperial bright as yon snow-white cloud:
Through many a room he passes lightly, swift;
Yet stops not long to see their grandeur proud;
But hastens on the stately stairway up,
Which shines with burnished gold all over steep:
There stands the lord—the emperor’s son—
To ask this lady what she does here alone.

The little one has made her own bed;
She puts it on with pillows white as new bread;
But when she sleeps there every night is dread,
For what should come at midnight from afar—
There comes no bird in time for any care!

There are some in whose heart Nature has poured
Sweet liquors from her own deep store;
Their lives have been as flowers which never fade,
As streams without a muddy tide;
They make us feel how kind our country is,
To such as live in it beside!

You see, I never had my mother’s milk;
When she died I was still very small;

But, oh how well it tastes in the heart-felt
way, when the ship is sailing fast!

The goodly man was clothed in white woollen stuff,

And shod with sandals that were yellowed all o'er.

His food is the milk of the ewe-lambs, and cheese;

For to him it pleaseth not to eat flesh.

He hath no care for gold or silver store;

But only loves his brethren, and their poor.*

In Rome I lived among women of great fame;

I ate sweet-smelling meats in luxury's flame;

There was no restraint on me, but wine or lust;

The slave who served me had not strength to resist.*

What are these verses about ? Who wrote them ? What is their tone ?*

